{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task SE-PE-01 requires me to implement a `solve(case)` function that processes a tree of nodes and returns which nodes are removed vs retained.\n\nLooking at the fixtures:\n- Each case has a list of nodes forming a tree\n- Each node has: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n- There's a `required_symbols` list\n\nThe output is:\n- `removed_node_ids`: list of node ids that are removed\n- `retained_node_ids`: list of node ids that are retained\n\nLet me think about what the logic should be. The task is \"SE-PE-01\" which seems to be about pruning/elimination in some structured tree.\n\nKey observations:\n1. Nodes have `removable` flag - non-removable nodes cannot be removed\n2. Nodes have `utility` values\n3. There are `required_symbols` - symbols that must be present in the retained tree\n\nThe task seems to be: remove nodes from a tree subject to constraints, likely to maximize or optimize some objective.\n\nLet me think about the constraints:\n1. A node can only be removed if `removable=true`\n2. If a node is removed, all its descendants should also be removed (tree structure)\n3. `required_symbols` must be covered - at least one node with each required symbol must be retained\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The `required_symbols` in the fixtures are `[\"root\", \"s1\"]`. All three cases have the same required symbols.\n\nIn case 000:\n- root: symbol=\"root\", removable=false\n- n1: symbol=\"s1\", removable=false\n- n2: symbol=\"s2\", removable=true\n- n3: symbol=\"s0\", removable=false\n- n4: parent=n2, symbol=\"s1\", removable=true\n- n5: parent=n3, symbol=\"s2\", removable=false\n- n6: parent=n1, symbol=\"s0\", removable=true\n\nRequired symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nNon-removable nodes: root, n1, n3, n5\nThese must be retained.\n\nn2 is removable - can we remove it? If we remove n2, n4 (its child) also gets removed.\nn6 is removable - can we remove it?\nn4 is removable - can we remove it?\n\nRequired symbols coverage:\n- \"root\" is covered by the \"root\" node (non-removable, always retained)\n- \"s1\" is covered by n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable)\n\nSince n1 covers \"s1\" and is non-removable, we don't need n4. So we could remove n4 and n2.\n\nAlso n6 (symbol=\"s0\") is removable and \"s0\" is not in required_symbols. But even if it were, n3 (non-removable) also has \"s0\".\n\nSo it seems like the optimization would be to remove all removable nodes that aren't needed to satisfy required_symbols, or perhaps maximize utility while satisfying required_symbols.\n\nWait, let me think more carefully. The task might be to remove nodes to minimize some cost or maximize utility while keeping all required symbols covered.\n\nActually, looking at this from a different angle - maybe the task is simply:\n1. Remove all nodes that are `removable=true` AND whose symbol is already covered by a non-removable node\n2. Or some pruning optimization\n\nOr perhaps the goal is to find the minimal set of nodes to keep such that all required_symbols are covered, keeping non-removable nodes, and if removing a parent also removes its subtree.\n\nLet me think about a greedy approach:\n- Keep all non-removable nodes (forced)\n- For required symbols not covered by non-removable nodes, keep the removable node with highest utility\n- Remove all other removable nodes (and their subtrees if the parent is removed)\n\nActually wait - if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree. But what if the parent is retained? Then we might optionally remove a child.\n\nLet me re-read the problem. It says \"pruning\" (PE = Pruning/Elimination?).\n\nFor case 000:\n- Required: root, s1\n- root is covered by the \"root\" node (non-removable) ✓\n- s1 is covered by n1 (non-removable) ✓\n- Since all required symbols are covered by non-removable nodes, all removable nodes can be removed\n- Removable nodes: n2, n4, n6\n- But wait - if n2 is removed, n4 (child of n2) is also removed. n4 is already removable.\n- n6 is removable and its parent n1 is retained\n\nSo removed: n2, n4, n6\nRetained: root, n1, n3, n5\n\nFor case 001:\nSame required_symbols: root, s1\n- root covered by root node ✓\n- s1 covered by n1 (non-removable) ✓\n- All removable nodes can be removed: n2, n4, n6\n- Retained: root, n1, n3, n5\n\nFor case 002:\nSame pattern - same result.\n\nHmm, but this seems too simple. Let me reconsider.\n\nMaybe the logic is: remove removable nodes while ensuring required symbols remain covered, trying to maximize total utility of retained nodes, or minimize the number of nodes removed, or something else.\n\nActually, maybe it's just: keep non-removable nodes and nodes that are necessary to maintain coverage of required symbols (nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols and no non-removable node covers that symbol).\n\nLet me think about a case where a required symbol is ONLY covered by removable nodes...\n\nFor example if required_symbols = [\"s2\"] and the only nodes with symbol \"s2\" are removable. Then we'd need to keep at least one of them.\n\nOK so the algorithm might be:\n1. All non-removable nodes are retained\n2. For each required symbol, if it's not covered by any retained node, add the removable node with highest utility that has that symbol\n3. Remove all removable nodes that are descendants of removed nodes\n4. All other removable nodes (that aren't needed for coverage) are removed\n\nBut there's a tree constraint: if you remove a parent, all children are removed. But if you keep a parent, you can still optionally remove its children.\n\nWait, actually I need to think about this more carefully. In the tree:\n- If a node is removed, all its descendants are removed (forced)\n- Non-removable nodes cannot be removed\n- Removable nodes can be removed unless their removal would cause a required symbol to become uncovered\n\nSo the question is: for each removable node, should it be removed?\n\nA removable node should be RETAINED if:\n- It has a required symbol that isn't covered by any other retained node\n\nA removable node is REMOVED if:\n- Its ancestor is removed (cascading removal), OR\n- It doesn't have a required symbol, OR  \n- Its required symbol is covered by another retained node\n\nActually I think the simpler interpretation might be correct:\n- Remove all removable nodes that can be removed while keeping required symbols covered\n- Non-removable nodes are always retained\n- Cascading: if a parent is removed, its children are removed\n\nLet me think about the order of operations:\n1. Build the tree\n2. Mark all non-removable nodes as retained\n3. Check which required symbols are already covered by retained nodes\n4. For uncovered required symbols, we need to keep some removable nodes\n5. For each uncovered required symbol, keep the removable node with highest utility (and make sure its ancestors are retained too)\n6. Remove all other removable nodes\n\nBut wait - if we need to keep a removable node, we also need to ensure its parent is retained. What if its parent is also removable?\n\nLet me think about this more carefully with a bottom-up approach.\n\nActually, since all cases in the fixtures have the same required_symbols [\"root\", \"s1\"] and \"root\" is covered by the non-removable root node, and \"s1\" is covered by non-removable n1, we'll always get:\n- retained: root, n1, n3, n5 (all non-removable)\n- removed: n2, n4, n6 (all removable)\n\nLet me implement this algorithm:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build node map\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Step 1: All non-removable nodes are retained\n    retained = set()\n    for n in nodes:\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            retained.add(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Step 2: Check which required symbols are covered\n    covered_symbols = set(node_map[nid][\"symbol\"] for nid in retained)\n    uncovered = required_symbols - covered_symbols\n    \n    # Step 3: For uncovered required symbols, find the best removable node\n    # (highest utility), and retain it along with its ancestor path\n    for sym in uncovered:\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if candidates:\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n            # Retain this node and all its ancestors\n            nid = best[\"id\"]\n            while nid is not None:\n                retained.add(nid)\n                nid = node_map[nid][\"parent\"]\n    \n    # Step 4: For retained removable nodes, check if their ancestors are retained\n    # (a removable node can only be retained if its parent is retained)\n    # This is already handled above\n    \n    # Step 5: A removable node should be removed if:\n    # - It's removable AND not in retained, OR\n    # - Its parent is removed (cascade)\n    \n    # Actually let's do a tree traversal to determine final status\n    # A node is removed if:\n    # - It's removable and not needed (not in our retained set), OR\n    # - Its parent is removed\n    \n    # BFS from root to determine final retained/removed status\n    from collections import deque\n    \n    final_retained = set()\n    final_removed = set()\n    \n    # Find root\n    root_id = next(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if n[\"parent\"] is None)\n    \n    queue = deque([root_id])\n    while queue:\n        nid = queue.popleft()\n        node = node_map[nid]\n        \n        # Check if parent is removed\n        parent_id = node[\"parent\"]\n        if parent_id is not None and parent_id in final_removed:\n            final_removed.add(nid)\n        elif nid in retained or not node[\"removable\"]:\n            final_retained.add(nid)\n            queue.extend(children[nid])\n        else:\n            # Removable and not in retained set\n            final_removed.add(nid)\n            # Children are also removed (cascade)\n            # Don't need to add to queue since they'll be removed\n            # But we need to mark them as removed\n            # Let's do it via cascade\n            child_queue = deque(children[nid])\n            while child_queue:\n                child_id = child_queue.popleft()\n                final_removed.add(child_id)\n                child_queue.extend(children[child_id])\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": sorted(final_removed),\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(final_retained)\n    }\n```\n\nWait, there's an issue with the BFS approach. Let me simplify:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Step 1: Non-removable nodes are always retained\n    must_retain = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"])\n    \n    # Step 2: Check coverage of required symbols\n    covered = set(node_map[nid][\"symbol\"] for nid in must_retain)\n    uncovered = required_symbols - covered\n    \n    # Step 3: For uncovered symbols, retain best candidate and its ancestors\n    extra_retain = set()\n    for sym in uncovered:\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if candidates:\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n            nid = best[\"id\"]\n            while nid is not None:\n                extra_retain.add(nid)\n                nid = node_map[nid][\"parent\"]\n    \n    retain_set = must_retain | extra_retain\n    \n    # Step 4: Cascade removals - if a node's parent is not retained, it's removed\n    # Do a top-down pass\n    final_retained = set()\n    final_removed = set()\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth to process top-down\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: n[\"depth\"])\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        parent_id = n[\"parent\"]\n        \n        if parent_id is not None and parent_id in final_removed:\n            # Parent was removed, so this node is also removed\n            final_removed.add(nid)\n        elif nid in retain_set:\n            final_retained.add(nid)\n        elif n[\"removable\"]:\n            # Removable and not in retain_set\n            final_removed.add(nid)\n        else:\n            # Non-removable (shouldn't happen as all non-removable are in retain_set)\n            final_retained.add(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": list(final_removed),\n        \"retained_node_ids\": list(final_retained)\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks cleaner. Let me trace through case 000:\n\nNodes:\n- root: non-removable → must_retain\n- n1: non-removable → must_retain\n- n2: removable\n- n3: non-removable → must_retain\n- n4: removable, parent=n2\n- n5: non-removable → must_retain\n- n6: removable, parent=n1\n\nmust_retain = {root, n1, n3, n5}\ncovered symbols from must_retain: root, s1, s0, s2\nrequired_symbols = {root, s1}\nuncovered = {} (empty - both are covered)\n\nextra_retain = {}\nretain_set = {root, n1, n3, n5}\n\nProcessing top-down (by depth):\n- root (depth 0): in retain_set → retained\n- n1 (depth 1): in retain_set → retained\n- n2 (depth 1): removable, not in retain_set → removed\n- n3 (depth 1): in retain_set → retained\n- n4 (depth 2): parent=n2, n2 is removed → removed\n- n5 (depth 2): in retain_set → retained\n- n6 (depth 2): removable, not in retain_set → removed\n\nfinal_retained = {root, n1, n3, n5}\nfinal_removed = {n2, n4, n6}\n\nThis seems right! The required symbols are covered, and all removable unnecessary nodes are pruned.\n\nLet me also verify my logic makes sense semantically: we want to prune the tree as much as possible while ensuring required symbols remain covered. Non-removable nodes are always kept. We only keep removable nodes if needed to cover a required symbol.\n\nThe implementation looks good. Let me clean it up and format it properly for the JSON response.\n\nActually, I should not sort the output - the schema just says array of strings, no ordering specified. But let me use sorted() for determinism.\n\nActually wait - should I sort? The schema doesn't require a specific order. Let me just return lists in some consistent order. Using sorted() by node id seems fine.\n\nLet me write the final implementation:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Non-removable nodes are always retained\n    must_retain = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes if not n[\"removable\"])\n    \n    # Check which required symbols are already covered by non-removable nodes\n    covered = set(node_map[nid][\"symbol\"] for nid in must_retain)\n    uncovered = required_symbols - covered\n    \n    # For each uncovered required symbol, retain the highest-utility removable\n    # candidate and ensure its ancestors are also retained\n    extra_retain = set()\n    for sym in uncovered:\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\"symbol\"] == sym and n[\"removable\"]]\n        if candidates:\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\"utility\"])\n            nid = best[\"id\"]\n            while nid is not None:\n                extra_retain.add(nid)\n                nid = node_map[nid].get(\"parent\")\n    \n    retain_set = must_retain | extra_retain\n    \n    # Top-down pass: cascade removals (removed parent =\u003e removed children)\n    final_retained = set()\n    final_removed = set()\n    \n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: n[\"depth\"])\n    \n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        parent_id = n.get(\"parent\")\n        \n        if parent_id is not None and parent_id in final_removed:\n            final_removed.add(nid)\n        elif nid in retain_set:\n            final_retained.add(nid)\n        else:\n            final_removed.add(nid)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": sorted(final_removed),\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(final_retained)\n    }\n```\n\nThis looks correct. Now I need to format it as a JSON string in the response envelope.\n\nThe response must be:\n```json\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n```\n\nLet me encode the Python code properly as a JSON string.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n\\n    # Non-removable nodes are always retained\\n    must_retain = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes if not n[\\\"removable\\\"])\\n\\n    # Determine which required symbols are already covered by non-removable nodes\\n    covered = set(node_map[nid][\\\"symbol\\\"] for nid in must_retain)\\n    uncovered = required_symbols - covered\\n\\n    # For each uncovered required symbol, retain the highest-utility removable\\n    # candidate and all ancestors on its path to the root\\n    extra_retain = set()\\n    for sym in uncovered:\\n        candidates = [n for n in nodes if n[\\\"symbol\\\"] == sym and n[\\\"removable\\\"]]\\n        if candidates:\\n            best = max(candidates, key=lambda n: n[\\\"utility\\\"])\\n            nid = best[\\\"id\\\"]\\n            while nid is not None:\\n                extra_retain.add(nid)\\n                nid = node_map[nid].get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n\\n    retain_set = must_retain | extra_retain\\n\\n    # Top-down pass (by depth): cascade removal from parent to children\\n    final_retained = set()\\n    final_removed = set()\\n\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: n[\\\"depth\\\"])\\n\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        parent_id = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n\\n        if parent_id is not None and parent_id in final_removed:\\n            # Parent was removed; cascade removal to this child\\n            final_removed.add(nid)\\n        elif nid in retain_set:\\n            final_retained.add(nid)\\n        else:\\n            # Removable and not needed for required-symbol coverage\\n            final_removed.add(nid)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": sorted(final_removed),\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": sorted(final_retained)\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_0147eKmSjw1opEKzWMtJgbdZ","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2216},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2216,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":61,"output_tokens":4968,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4440},"service_tier":"standard"}}